Fresh-context documentation accuracy review. Report only; do NOT edit any file. Project: D:\WorkEnv\ecommerce-support-agent (Python 3.13). Run Python only via `.venv/Scripts/python` from the project root (Bash tool, POSIX syntax).

Goal: verify that every documentation claim matches the actual codebase, and that the docs describe the architecture, structure and code thoroughly enough. Do not trust any doc; check each claim against the code (read files, grep, run commands).

Docs to check: README.md; docs/design.md (all sections, including §14 revisions — later sections override earlier ones, but flag earlier statements that are now false and not overridden); docs/decisions.md; docs/adr/*.md and docs/adr/README.md; docs/threat-model.md; docs/production-architecture.md; docs/analysis.md (only the "Revisions after the design reviews" notes and whether Candidate decisions are reflected or explicitly superseded); docs/eval-report.md (regenerate with `.venv/Scripts/python -m support_agent run-scenarios --report <temp path outside repo>` and diff against the committed one); docs/brief.md (only factual errors); config/policy.json "note"; .env.example; docstrings of public modules in src/support_agent.

For each doc check, among others:
- module/file names, class and function names, signatures, enum members, reason codes, tool names and tool argument fields, result fields, config fields and default values, file paths, the folder tree in decisions.md §3 (flag modules listed that don't exist or exist but aren't listed, unless the doc says design.md wins);
- behaviour claims: policy rule order and semantics (cumulative threshold, customer cap, order cap, dispute rule incl. order-total exclusion), confirmation contract, idempotency key composition, refund states, output-check rules, claims extraction rules, mock model method and limits (intent keywords and precedence, LIST intent, quantity rule, ambiguity rule, item matching, status reply), escalation summary format, audit fields (prompt_version, policy_version, model_id), layer contracts (compare with pyproject [tool.importlinter]), ruff rules (compare with pyproject), scripts.check steps and order (compare with scripts/check.py), test/coverage numbers if stated, counts (ADRs, adversarial cases, subagent transcripts, tests);
- README commands: each exists and works as described (run the non-install ones);
- threat-model: each cited test exists and actually tests the stated control;
- numbers and examples in README (amounts per scenario, latency figure if any) match a real run.

Output: a list of findings grouped by document, each with: the doc location (file:line), the claim, what the code actually does (file:line evidence), severity (wrong / outdated / missing / unclear), and the exact correction. Then a short list of important code or architecture aspects that NO doc describes. No praise; max 40 findings, most important first.

